@*
    One diagram, drawn twice: the mermaid source rendered live on the left, and the
    Archify document authored from the same facts on the right.

    Chrome, not a component. It exists only on the comparison page, and the whole
    point of that page is being able to tell the two renderers apart — so each pane
    is captioned with the name of the tool that drew it. A pair of unlabelled
    pictures would leave a reader guessing which half of the comparison they are
    looking at, which is the one thing the page must not do.

    The grid is `.sb-columns`, the same auto-fit track every other side-by-side on
    this site uses. It collapses to one column below its minimum, so the pair
    stacks on a narrow viewport without this component knowing anything about
    breakpoints.

    The right pane is an iframe because an Archify artifact is not a fragment we
    can drop into the page: it is a whole HTML document — its own stylesheet, its
    own theme attribute on `<html>`, its own viewer runtime — and rendering it
    inline would mean its 675 KB of CSS competing with the storybook's. A document
    stays a document; the frame is the only honest way to show one.

    `loading="lazy"` is not an optimisation detail, it is what makes three of these
    on one page tolerable: each artifact is roughly 675 KB, and fetching all of
    them before the first paint would cost two megabytes to look at the top story.

    On `sandbox`: `allow-scripts` alone puts the frame in an opaque origin, where
    `localStorage` throws. Archify's theme script guards every access to it, so the
    viewer survives that and keeps the frame from reaching back into the storybook.
    Scripts have to stay allowed — without them the artifact's viewer never runs and
    the frame renders an unstyled skeleton.

    `?theme=dark` is why the pane sits flush with the page instead of being a white
    card on it. The artifact resolves its own theme before first paint, and with
    `localStorage` unavailable in that opaque origin it falls back to the reader's
    `prefers-color-scheme` — so a reader on a light system got Archify's light
    palette inside a product that `.design/design-principles.md` makes dark-mode-only,
    and the comparison turned into a lighting difference. The parameter is the
    document's own published lever, read from the query string, so pinning it is done
    entirely from out here: nothing in the generated HTML is touched, and a
    regeneration cannot undo it.
*@

<div class="sb-columns" data-testid="@TestId">
    <div class="sb-stack">
        <p class="sb-label">Mermaid</p>
        <DiagramView Title="@Title" Language="mermaid" Source="@Mermaid" />
    </div>

    <div class="sb-stack">
        <p class="sb-label">Archify</p>
        <iframe class="sb-archify-frame"
                src="@($"{ArchifyArtifact}?theme=dark")"
                title="@($"{Title}, drawn by Archify")"
                loading="lazy"
                sandbox="allow-scripts"
                data-testid="@($"{TestId}-archify")"></iframe>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The mermaid the left pane draws. The same string goes to the
    /// story's <c>Source</c>, because a comparison is only worth anything if the
    /// reader can check what both sides were given.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Mermaid { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The committed Archify document, as a path under <c>wwwroot</c>.
    /// A generated artifact rather than a rendering: nothing here converts the
    /// mermaid above, and the two are kept in step by hand.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string ArchifyArtifact { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>What the diagram is called. It captions the mermaid pane and names
    /// the frame, so it is also the accessible name a screen reader announces for
    /// the Archify document.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Title { get; set; } = "Diagram";

    /// <summary>Addresses the pair from a test. The frame gets the same id with an
    /// <c>-archify</c> suffix, so a test can assert on the document without having
    /// to guess at a second identifier.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }
}
